---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
---

<Article
  title="What a tutorial will look like here"
  description="A sample lesson demonstrating the reading layout — the notebook margin, prose rhythm, code, and asides."
  datePublished={new Date('2026-08-08')}
  dateUpdated={new Date('2026-08-08')}
  readingMinutes={4}
  noindex
>
  <h2>Why the page looks like this</h2>
  <p>
    This site is built for reading. One calm column, around sixty-five
    characters wide — the width where your eye finds the next line without
    effort. On a wide screen, dates and metadata sit in the left margin, like
    the edge of a paper notebook. On your phone, they fold above the title.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beginner-friendly means the writing assumes nothing, not that it decorates
    everything. Short paragraphs. One idea each. Plain words where plain words
    work.
  </p>

  <h2>Code you can actually read</h2>
  <p>
    Code blocks get their own quiet surface, and later a copy button and line
    highlighting:
  </p>
  <pre><code>const greet = (name) =&gt; `Namaste, $&lbrace;name&rbrace;!`;

console.log(greet('learner'));
// Namaste, learner!</code></pre>

  <h2>Asides that don't interrupt</h2>
  <blockquote>
    Anything that would break the flow — a deeper dive, a warning, a story —
    steps politely to the side like this, or folds away until you want it.
  </blockquote>
</Article>
